    "summary": "Epic Playing‑In‑The‑Band sandwich, smoking Stranger, tight jams",
    "blurb": "The first set launches with a fiery Feel Like A Stranger and keeps the energy high through Candyman, Cassidy, Althea and Hell In A Bucket. The second set erupts into an expansive Playing In The Band sandwich that flows into Uncle John's Band and a soaring China Doll, before a powerful Throwing Stones > Not Fade Away climax and a heartfelt All Over Now Baby Blue encore.",
    "review": "October ’84 at Meadowlands finds the Dead in top‑form, delivering one of the most rewarding fall shows of the era. The opening Stranger is described as “smoking” across both soundboard and audience tapes, while Jerry’s voice shows a hint of strain but his guitar work stays “magic fingers” strong throughout. Phil locks down the low end with looping, rafter‑shaking bass lines that drive the jams, and Brent’s synth/Healy textures shine especially during the China Doll excursion. The rhythm section—Mickey and Billy—provides energetic fills and a tight pre‑drums jam that fuels the second‑set marathon.\n\nThe highlight is undoubtedly the massive Playing In The Band sandwich: it rolls into Uncle John's Band, blossoms into a hypnotic China Doll, and later resurfaces before launching into a blistering Throwing Stones > Not Fade Away sequence. The show closes on an emotionally resonant All Over Now Baby Blue encore that feels both cathartic and crystal‑clear thanks to the standout soundboard capture. For anyone hunting a quintessential mid‑’80s Dead experience, this performance balances high‑octane improvisation with tight songcraft, making it a must‑listen.",
